  "courseName": "General Chemistry I",
  "estimatedLength": "30 minutes",
  "objectives": [
    "Determine the steric number of a central atom from its Lewis structure by counting bonding pairs and lone pairs.",
    "Predict the electron-domain geometry of a molecule from its steric number.",
    "Distinguish electron-domain geometry from molecular geometry when lone pairs are present.",
    "Explain why lone pairs occupy more angular space than bonding pairs and predict bond-angle deviations.",
    "Apply VSEPR predictions to specific molecules and compare them to experimentally observed structures."
  ],
  "modules": [
    {
      "moduleType": "demo",
      "prefab": "demoPrefab",
      "moduleName": "Fundamental Geometries & Lone Pair Repulsion",
      "description": "Exploration of steric numbers 2, 3, and 4, illustrating linear, trigonal planar, and tetrahedral shapes, and how lone pairs affect bond angles.",
      "author": "Dr. Evelyn Vance",
      "institution": "University Department of Chemistry",
      "dateCreated": "2023-10-15",
      "educationalObjectives": [
        "Explain the fundamental driving force of VSEPR: minimizing electron domain repulsion.",
        "Demonstrate how 4 electron domains arrange in 3D space to form a tetrahedron.",
        "Contrast the tetrahedral geometry of methane with the bent geometry of water.",
        "Explain why water's bond angle is reduced from 109.5 to 104.5 degrees due to lone pair repulsion."
      ],
      "instructions": [
        "Stand in an open area with a tabletop workspace in front of you.",
        "Observe the 3D molecular structures as they appear in your AR space.",
        "Listen to the narration to understand how electron domains repel one another to dictate molecular geometry."
